<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madrasa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A madrasa is an Islamic institution of higher learning, typically teaching law, theology and related sciences. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, notably under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, founded influential madrasas to train scholars and officials and promote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> continued this policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madrasa.jpg" alt="Madrasa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain; derivative work by MrPanyGoff · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
